__d(function(e,t,a,i,o,s,n){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:9,chapter_title:"The Living Organisms and Their Surroundings",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is a habitat?",options:["A) The food an organism eats","B) The natural surroundings where an organism lives","C) The way an organism moves","D) The body structure of an organism"],correct_answer:"B) The natural surroundings where an organism lives",explanation:"A habitat is the natural environment where an organism lives, finds food, reproduces, and interacts with other living things. It includes both living (biotic) and non-living (abiotic) components. Examples: pond for fish, forest for tiger, desert for camel."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is an abiotic component of a habitat?",options:["A) Fish","B) Water plants","C) Sunlight","D) Frogs"],correct_answer:"C) Sunlight",explanation:"Abiotic components are non-living factors in a habitat. Sunlight is a non-living physical factor that affects all organisms. Fish, water plants, and frogs are living organisms \u2014 they are biotic components. Other abiotic factors include water, temperature, soil, and air."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The hump of a camel stores:",options:["A) Water","B) Fat","C) Blood","D) Air"],correct_answer:"B) Fat",explanation:"The camel's hump stores fat, not water. The fat serves as an energy reserve that the camel can metabolise when food is scarce in the desert. This is a common misconception \u2014 people believe the hump stores water, but it is actually fat. Water is stored in the camel's bloodstream and tissues."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which plant part is modified into spines in a cactus?",options:["A) Stem","B) Root","C) Leaf","D) Flower"],correct_answer:"C) Leaf",explanation:"In a cactus, the leaves are modified into spines. This adaptation reduces the surface area through which water can evaporate, conserving water in the desert. The thick, fleshy green stem takes over the function of photosynthesis in the cactus plant."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is an adaptation that helps fish live in water?",options:["A) Wings","B) Thick fur","C) Gills","D) Hump"],correct_answer:"C) Gills",explanation:"Gills are the organs fish use to extract dissolved oxygen from water for breathing. This is a key aquatic adaptation. Wings are for flying (birds), thick fur is for cold climates (polar animals), and a hump is for desert life (camel). Gills allow fish to breathe underwater without access to air."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does MRS GREN stand for in biology?",options:["A) The name of a famous biologist","B) The seven characteristics of living organisms","C) The five kingdoms of life","D) Types of habitats"],correct_answer:"B) The seven characteristics of living organisms",explanation:"MRS GREN is a mnemonic for the seven characteristics shared by all living organisms: Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity (response to stimuli), Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, and Nutrition. All living things show all seven characteristics \u2014 non-living things do not."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The Western Ghats in Karnataka is known as:",options:["A) A desert region","B) A biodiversity hotspot","C) A polar habitat","D) An aquatic habitat"],correct_answer:"B) A biodiversity hotspot",explanation:"The Western Ghats is one of the world's 36 biodiversity hotspots \u2014 a region with exceptionally high numbers of species, many endemic (found nowhere else), but under serious threat. It passes through Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Goa, and Maharashtra and is home to thousands of plant and animal species."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a terrestrial animal?",options:["A) Dolphin","B) Salmon","C) Elephant","D) Octopus"],correct_answer:"C) Elephant",explanation:"An elephant is a terrestrial (land-dwelling) animal \u2014 it lives and moves on land. Dolphins and octopuses are aquatic marine animals, and salmon are aquatic freshwater/marine fish. Terrestrial animals breathe air directly, have limbs for movement on land, and are adapted to land-based habitats like forests and grasslands."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A plant that lives in water and has air spaces in its stem for buoyancy is called a:",options:["A) Xerophyte","B) Hydrophyte","C) Mesophyte","D) Epiphyte"],correct_answer:"B) Hydrophyte",explanation:"A hydrophyte is a plant adapted to living in water or very wet conditions. Hydrophytes have features like air spaces (aerenchyma) in stems for buoyancy and oxygen supply, flexible stems, and floating or submerged leaves. Examples: lotus, water lily, hydrilla. Xerophyte = desert plant; mesophyte = plants in moderate conditions; epiphyte = plants growing on other plants."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which adaptation helps a mountain tree shed snow from its branches?",options:["A) Broad, flat leaves","B) Needle-shaped leaves and conical shape","C) No leaves at all","D) Underground stems"],correct_answer:"B) Needle-shaped leaves and conical shape",explanation:"Mountain trees like pine and fir have needle-shaped leaves (to reduce water loss and snow accumulation) and a conical/triangular body shape. The sloping sides allow snow to slide off easily, preventing branches from breaking under heavy snow load. This is a classic structural adaptation to mountain habitats."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A kangaroo rat survives in the desert without drinking water. How?",options:["A) It stores water in its tail","B) It gets water from the seeds it eats through metabolic processes","C) It absorbs water through its skin","D) It drinks dew from leaves at night"],correct_answer:"B) It gets water from the seeds it eats through metabolic processes",explanation:"The kangaroo rat obtains water metabolically \u2014 seeds contain carbohydrates which, when broken down during respiration, produce water (called metabolic water). It also conserves water by being nocturnal (active only at night to avoid daytime heat), excreting highly concentrated urine, and having very efficient kidneys. It never needs to drink liquid water."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the role of decomposers in a habitat?",options:["A) They produce food through photosynthesis","B) They eat other animals","C) They break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil","D) They provide oxygen to aquatic organisms"],correct_answer:"C) They break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil",explanation:"Decomposers (mainly bacteria and fungi) break down dead plants and animals into simpler chemical substances, returning vital nutrients to the soil. This process (decomposition) completes the nutrient cycle and makes minerals available for plants to absorb again. Without decomposers, dead matter would accumulate and nutrients would not be recycled."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following is a behavioural adaptation?",options:["A) Webbed feet in ducks","B) Thick fur in polar bears","C) Migration of birds in winter","D) Spines in cactus"],correct_answer:"C) Migration of birds in winter",explanation:"Migration is a behavioural adaptation \u2014 it is an action/behaviour (long-distance seasonal movement) rather than a physical body feature. Webbed feet, thick fur, and spines are all structural (physical/anatomical) adaptations. Behavioural adaptations include hibernation, nocturnal activity, and flocking. Both structural and behavioural adaptations help organisms survive in their habitat."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which feature of a lotus plant helps it float on water?",options:["A) Deep roots anchored in mud","B) Large waxy leaves and air-filled stem tissues","C) Needle-shaped leaves","D) Thick, fleshy stem storing water"],correct_answer:"B) Large waxy leaves and air-filled stem tissues",explanation:"The lotus floats because of: (1) large, flat, waxy leaves that spread on the water surface (wax repels water, keeping leaves dry and providing a platform); (2) air-filled tissues (aerenchyma) in stems and stalks that provide buoyancy. The lotus is Karnataka's state flower and a classic hydrophyte. Its roots are anchored in mud, but the plant floats on the surface."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following pairs correctly matches an organism with its habitat type?",options:["A) Camel \u2014 aquatic habitat","B) Lotus \u2014 desert habitat","C) Polar bear \u2014 mountain habitat","D) Fish \u2014 aquatic habitat"],correct_answer:"D) Fish \u2014 aquatic habitat",explanation:"Fish live in water \u2014 they are aquatic organisms adapted for underwater life with gills, fins, and streamlined bodies. Camels live in deserts (terrestrial/arid habitat), lotus grows in ponds and lakes (aquatic habitat, not desert), and polar bears live in polar/Arctic regions (not mountains). Matching organisms to their correct habitat type is a key skill in this chapter."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A frog can live both on land and in water. This type of habitat is called:",options:["A) Terrestrial","B) Aquatic","C) Amphibious","D) Arboreal"],correct_answer:"C) Amphibious",explanation:"An amphibious habitat supports organisms that can live both on land and in water. Frogs are the classic example \u2014 they breathe through lungs on land and through moist skin/gills in water; they breed in water but hunt on land. The word 'amphibian' literally means 'double life' (amphi = both, bios = life). Arboreal means tree-dwelling."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why do polar bears have thick white fur?",options:["A) White fur reflects sunlight to keep them cool","B) Thick fur provides insulation against extreme cold; white colour provides camouflage in snow","C) White fur helps them attract mates","D) Fur keeps them dry when swimming"],correct_answer:"B) Thick fur provides insulation against extreme cold; white colour provides camouflage in snow",explanation:"Polar bears have thick fur with two layers \u2014 a dense undercoat for insulation (trapping body heat) and a thicker outer coat. The white colour camouflages them in the snowy Arctic environment, helping them hunt seals. They also have a thick layer of fat (blubber) under the skin for extra insulation. These are adaptations to the cold polar habitat."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A scientist finds a plant with: thick fleshy stem, leaves modified into spines, waxy coating on stem, and stomata that open only at night. In which habitat does this plant most likely live?",options:["A) Pond","B) Dense rainforest","C) Hot dry desert","D) Snowy mountain"],correct_answer:"C) Hot dry desert",explanation:"All four features described are xerophyte (desert plant) adaptations: (1) thick fleshy stem = stores water; (2) spiny leaves = reduced surface area, less water lost, protection; (3) waxy coating = prevents water evaporation from stem; (4) stomata open only at night = avoids water loss during hot daytime. Together, these identify this as a classic desert plant like a cactus. No other habitat requires all four of these water-conservation strategies simultaneously."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Two different bird species live in the same forest and eat different types of food (one eats insects from bark, the other eats fruits). Which ecological concept does this illustrate?",options:["A) Hibernation","B) Niche differentiation allowing coexistence","C) Predator-prey relationship","D) Migration"],correct_answer:"B) Niche differentiation allowing coexistence",explanation:"This illustrates niche differentiation \u2014 two species can share the same habitat (forest) if they occupy different ecological niches (different food sources, different foraging methods). This reduces competition between them and allows both to coexist. If two species occupied the exact same niche, one would outcompete and eliminate the other (competitive exclusion principle). Niche differentiation promotes biodiversity."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"If all decomposers were to disappear from a forest ecosystem, what would be the MOST LIKELY long-term consequence?",options:["A) Predators would increase in number","B) Plants would grow faster with more sunlight","C) Nutrients would be locked in dead matter, soil would become infertile, and plants would eventually die","D) Animals would adapt by eating dead matter themselves"],correct_answer:"C) Nutrients would be locked in dead matter, soil would become infertile, and plants would eventually die",explanation:"Decomposers recycle nutrients by breaking down dead organisms and returning minerals (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium etc.) to the soil. Without decomposers: dead matter would accumulate massively; nutrients would remain locked inside dead bodies; the soil would become nutrient-poor; plants would fail to grow; herbivores would starve; then carnivores would follow \u2014 the entire ecosystem would collapse. Decomposers are foundational to all nutrient cycles."}]}},2719,[]);